<article class="env-lemma" id="02FV">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02FV">Lemma <span data-tag="02FV">29.22.11</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of schemes over $S$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $X$ is locally of finite presentation over $S$ and $Y$ is locally of finite type over $S$, then $f$ is locally of finite presentation. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $X$ is of finite presentation over $S$ and $Y$ is quasi-separated and locally of finite type over $S$, then $f$ is of finite presentation. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Proof of (1). Via Lemma <a href="/tag/01TQ" data-tag="01TQ">29.22.2</a> this translates into the following algebra fact: Given ring maps $A \to B \to C$ such that $A \to C$ is of finite presentation and $A \to B$ is of finite type, then $B \to C$ is of finite presentation. See Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00F4" data-tag="00F4">10.6.2</a>. 
    <p>
      Part (2) follows from (1) and Schemes, Lemmas <a href="/tag/01KV" data-tag="01KV">26.21.13</a> and <a href="/tag/03GI" data-tag="03GI">26.21.14</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>